---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// Favorite quotes, newest first is not required: order is whatever reads best.
// To add one: { text: '...', author: '...', source?: '...' }
// `text` can be an array when several passages from the same person and occasion
// belong together: each becomes its own paragraph under one attribution.
// `source` is optional context (a book, talk, or paper) shown after the name, and
// links to `url` when one is given. Set `paraphrased` when the wording is recalled
// rather than transcribed, so the page never passes it off as verbatim.
// Each excerpt gets a shareable #anchor, slugged from `author`. A second quote by the
// same author fails the build; give that one an explicit `id` (short and memorable,
// e.g. id: 'karpathy-understanding') so the first one's shared link keeps working.
const quotes: {
  text: string | string[];
  author: string;
  source?: string;
  url?: string;
  paraphrased?: boolean;
  id?: string;
}[] = [
  {
    text: [
      'People have a misconception that it takes a lot of exceptional people to make frontier models. ' +
        'You need a handful of experienced people, but mostly you need a lot of motivated and talented people… ' +
        'This technology is not that old. The people who have experience like myself only have a few years of ' +
        'experience working on this. A lot of people can build that experience very quickly.',
      'It is good for the world if there are thousands of models, by different providers. ' +
        'Competition breeds innovation, and specialization is better than one size fits all. ' +
        'Different users want models at different sizes and different price points. Specifically for India, ' +
        'we have so many languages and cultures, and our own education, legal and political systems, our own ' +
        'public policy. So we want models that can be customized for all these things, and that is why we ' +
        'need to build these models here.',
    ],
    author: 'Devendra Singh Chaplot',
    source: 'at Sarvam Epoch',
    url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=peO2ReobYSw',
  },
  {
    text: [
      'With scaling laws, people realized that scaling pretraining is the thing to do. The big breakthrough ' +
        'of pretraining is the realization that this recipe is good: if you mix some data with some compute ' +
        'into a neural net of a certain size, you will get results, and you know you will get something ' +
        'better if you scale the recipe up.',
      'At some point though, pretraining will run out of data. The data is very finite. So then we either do ' +
        'souped up pretraining or some kind of RL, and in some sense we are back to the age of research.',
      'From 2012 to 2020, it was the age of research. From 2020 to 2025, it was the age of scaling. But now ' +
        'the scale is so big that the belief that things would be significantly different if it were even ' +
        '100x bigger might not be true. So we are back to the age of research again.',
    ],
    author: 'Ilya Sutskever',
    source: 'on the Dwarkesh Podcast',
    url: 'https://youtube.com/shorts/pLHWn5SQvLM',
    paraphrased: true,
  },
  {
    text: [
      'Current LLM agents can do really well and are incredibly capable when they are given a clear and ' +
        'detailed specification. With access to these kinds of agents, the scarce skill then becomes having ' +
        'incredibly good taste in what you ask them to work on.',
      'A researcher can have all the tools and techniques, but often most of the battle is what problem you ' +
        'are going to spend your time on. That high level wisdom of what to work on is incredibly important, ' +
        'and I think models are not necessarily going to be good at it. We are going to have people steering ' +
        'a lot of AI-assisted computation in order to accomplish great things more quickly, but the essence ' +
        'of what you want the models to do is the key thing to focus on.',
      'Building taste is difficult. Working on a lot of problems in the past teaches you what kind of ' +
        'problems might be interesting in future, and what might just be possible by cobbling together ' +
        'previous approaches. Another way is to write down a bunch of things you think might be important in ' +
        'the near future, pick one of them to work on, and after a certain amount of time go back and ' +
        'evaluate which of the others turned out to be important and worked for other people. That gives you ' +
        'a lot more samples to build taste from. Finally, do crazy thought experiments, and don’t take as ' +
        'given the things that most people seem to take as given.',
    ],
    author: 'Jeff Dean',
    source: '“The 1% Rule for Building in AI”',
    url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CxXgV54KzpQ',
    paraphrased: true,
  },
  {
    text: 'You insist that there is something a machine cannot do. If you tell me precisely what a ' +
      'machine cannot do, then I can always make a machine which will just do that.',
    author: 'John von Neumann',
  },
  {
    text: 'You can outsource your thinking but you cannot outsource your understanding.',
    author: 'Andrej Karpathy',
    source: 'commonly attributed',
  },
  {
    text: [
      'When you grow up you tend to get told the world is the way it is and your life is just to live ' +
        'your life inside the world. Try not to bash into the walls too much, try to have a nice family ' +
        'life, have fun, save a little money. But that is a very limited life, life can be much broader. ' +
        'Once you discover one simple fact, and that is everything you call life was made up by people ' +
        'that were no smarter than you. And you can change it, you can influence it. You can build your ' +
        'own things that other people can use. And the minute that you understand that you can poke life, ' +
        'and if you push in something will actually pop out the other side, that you can change and mold it.',
      'The most important thing is to shake off this erroneous notion that life is there and you are just ' +
        'going to live in it versus embrace it, change it, make your mark upon it. However you learn it, ' +
        'once you learn, you’ll want to change life and make it better because it is kind of messed up in ' +
        'a lot of ways. Once you learn that you will never be the same again.',
    ],
    author: 'Steve Jobs',
    paraphrased: true,
  },
  {
    text: [
      'In future people won’t necessarily be having shitty jobs or nothing to do. Instead they will be ' +
        'handling and guiding the Robots and AI. If we don’t get ASI, if AI gets you to expert level but ' +
        'doesn’t go beyond and still needs human taste, guidance and creativity, humans are still the ' +
        'motivated things in the society. People become like Pokémon trainers or robot/AI handlers.',
      'That is what we are seeing today. People who are using AI today are more productive than ever. ' +
        'The only way you are being displaced by AI if you are refusing to use it.',
    ],
    author: 'Naval Ravikant',
    source: '“Riding AGI, AI Anxiety, …”',
    url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6m-ZZBCiiEE',
  },
  {
    text: [
      'There will be 3 types of people in the future:',
      'The Purists - reject AI and become new-age hippies',
      'The Automatons - outsource every decision and become machines',
      'The New Renaissance Man - maintains humanity and leverages tech to do what used to be impossible',
    ],
    author: 'Dan Koe',
    source: 'on X',
    url: 'https://x.com/thedankoe/status/2091876986444533842',
  },
  {
    text: 'Computer Science is no more about computers than astronomy is about telescopes',
    author: 'Edsger Wybe Dijkstra',
    // Wikiquote files this under Disputed ("possibly folklore"); the longer version
    // with microscopes and beakers is documented to Fellows and Parberry, Computing
    // Research News, Jan 1993. Attribution kept as Dijkstra at the site owner's call.
    id: 'dijkstra',
  },
];

const slugify = (value: string) =>
  value
    .toLowerCase()
    .normalize('NFD')
    .replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g, '')
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')
    .replace(/^-|-$/g, '');

// Slugs are never derived from array position: a shared link has to keep pointing at
// the same excerpt no matter what gets added above it later. So a second quote by an
// author collides on purpose and fails the build, and you resolve it by pinning `id`
// on the NEW one, which leaves the already-shared link on the old one untouched.
const seen = new Map<string, string>();
const entries = quotes.map((q) => {
  const slug = q.id ?? slugify(q.author);
  const clash = seen.get(slug);
  if (clash) {
    throw new Error(
      `Duplicate excerpt anchor "#${slug}" (${clash} and ${q.author}). Add an explicit ` +
        `id to the newer quote, e.g. id: '${slug}-${slugify(
          (Array.isArray(q.text) ? q.text[0] : q.text).split(' ').slice(0, 3).join(' ')
        )}'. Do not renumber the existing one: its link is already out in the world.`
    );
  }
  seen.set(slug, q.author);
  return { ...q, slug };
});
---

<Base title="Excerpts · Varun Gandhi" description="Ideas about AI and technology, or by computer scientists, that I keep coming back to.">
  <h1>Excerpts</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    Ideas about AI and technology, or by computer scientists, that I keep coming back to.
  </p>
  {quotes.length === 0 ? (
    <p class="empty">Nothing here yet.</p>
  ) : (
    <ul class="quote-list">
      {entries.map((q) => (
        <li id={q.slug}>
          <blockquote>
            {(Array.isArray(q.text) ? q.text : [q.text]).map((para) => <p>{para}</p>)}
          </blockquote>
          <div class="meta">
            {/* kept on one line: a line break here renders as a space before the comma */}
            <p class="attribution">
              {q.author}{q.source && <span class="source">, {q.url ? <a href={q.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{q.source}</a> : q.source}</span>}{q.paraphrased && <span class="paraphrased"> (paraphrased)</span>}
            </p>
            <a class="share" href={`#${q.slug}`} data-slug={q.slug} aria-label={`Copy a link to this excerpt by ${q.author}`}>
              <svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                <path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71" />
                <path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71" />
              </svg>
              <span class="share-text">Copy link</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )}
  <script is:inline>
    // Fisher-Yates shuffle of the excerpts, run inline right after the list so it
    // reorders before first paint (same pre-paint trick as the theme script in
    // Base.astro). No-JS visitors simply get the authored order.
    (() => {
      const list = document.querySelector('.quote-list');
      if (!list) return;
      const items = Array.from(list.children);
      for (let i = items.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
        const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
        [items[i], items[j]] = [items[j], items[i]];
      }
      for (const item of items) list.append(item);
    })();

    // The shuffle above moves the element the browser already scrolled to, so a
    // shared #link lands at the wrong offset. Re-run the jump once layout settles.
    addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
      const id = decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1));
      if (id) document.getElementById(id)?.scrollIntoView({ block: 'center' });
    });

    // Share controls copy an absolute link. They stay real anchors, so with no JS
    // (or no clipboard access) clicking still jumps to the excerpt and puts the
    // shareable URL in the address bar.
    if (navigator.clipboard) {
      for (const btn of document.querySelectorAll('.share')) {
        btn.addEventListener('click', async (event) => {
          event.preventDefault();
          const slug = btn.dataset.slug;
          try {
            await navigator.clipboard.writeText(location.origin + location.pathname + '#' + slug);
          } catch {
            location.hash = slug;
            return;
          }
          const label = btn.querySelector('.share-text');
          btn.classList.add('copied');
          label.textContent = 'Copied';
          clearTimeout(btn.resetTimer);
          btn.resetTimer = setTimeout(() => {
            btn.classList.remove('copied');
            label.textContent = 'Copy link';
          }, 1600);
        });
      }
    }
  </script>
</Base>

<style>
  .intro {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .empty {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .quote-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .quote-list li {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    scroll-margin-top: 2rem;
    transition: border-color 0.15s ease, background-color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .quote-list li:hover {
    border-color: var(--text-muted);
    border-left-color: var(--link);
  }

  /* the excerpt someone was linked to, so a shared link lands somewhere obvious */
  .quote-list li:target {
    border-color: var(--text-muted);
    border-left-color: var(--link);
  }

  /* Base.astro styles blockquotes as muted pull-quotes with a left rule; on this
     page the quote *is* the content, so it gets body color and no rule. */
  blockquote {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 0;
    border-left: none;
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  blockquote p {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
  }

  blockquote p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
  }

  .attribution {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .share {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.4rem;
    border-radius: 6px;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 0.15s ease, background-color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .share:hover,
  .share:focus-visible {
    color: var(--link);
    background: var(--bg);
  }

  .share.copied,
  .share.copied:hover {
    color: var(--heading);
    background: var(--bg);
  }

  .share svg {
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .attribution::before {
    content: '\2014\00a0';
  }

  .source {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .paraphrased {
    font-style: normal;
  }
</style>
